Shadows are off by default. Three steps to turn them on:
// 1. Renderer
renderer.shadowMap.enabled = true;
renderer.shadowMap.type = THREE.PCFSoftShadowMap;
// 2. Lights that cast
const sun = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 1.0);
sun.position.set(10, 15, 10);
sun.castShadow = true;
sun.shadow.mapSize.set(2048, 2048);
sun.shadow.camera.near = 0.5;
sun.shadow.camera.far = 50;
sun.shadow.camera.left = -20;
sun.shadow.camera.right = 20;
sun.shadow.camera.top = 20;
sun.shadow.camera.bottom = -20;
sun.shadow.bias = -0.0005;
sun.shadow.normalBias = 0.02;
scene.add(sun);
scene.add(new THREE.CameraHelper(sun.shadow.camera)); // debug
// 3. Meshes that cast / receive
model.traverse((c) => {
if (c.isMesh) { c.castShadow = true; c.receiveShadow = true; }
});
floor.receiveShadow = true;
Forget any one of these three and you'll see no shadows.

A shadow-casting light renders the scene from its own viewpoint into a depth texture (the shadow map). That viewpoint is a camera — orthographic for DirectionalLight, perspective for SpotLight.
The frustum must:
Be as small as possible while enclosing every shadow caster.
Not crop the receiving floor / walls.
If too large → wasted precision → blocky shadows. If too small → shadows fade out where the frustum ends.
const cam = sun.shadow.camera;
cam.near = 1;
cam.far = 50;
cam.left = cam.bottom = -25;
cam.right = cam.top = 25;
cam.updateProjectionMatrix(); // required after changes
Trick: add scene.add(new THREE.CameraHelper(sun.shadow.camera)) while tuning. You see the shadow camera frustum in real time. Remove for production.

For huge outdoor terrains, a single shadow map is either pixelated everywhere (too big a frustum) or sharp near and gone far (too small). CSM splits the camera frustum into N slices (cascades), each with its own shadow map at decreasing resolution as distance grows.
Three.js doesn't ship CSM; use three-csm (or its r3f cousin react-three-csm):
npm install three-csm
import { CSM } from 'three-csm';
const csm = new CSM({
maxFar: 200,
cascades: 4,
shadowMapSize: 2048,
lightDirection: new THREE.Vector3(-1, -1, -1).normalize(),
camera: camera,
parent: scene,
});
csm.update(); // call each frame
// Materials must be updated to read the cascade uniforms:
mesh.material = csm.setupMaterial(mesh.material);
Use CSM whenever the visible terrain is much larger than the camera's close-up area — landscape games, open worlds, architectural visualisations of city blocks.

A plane that only displays the shadow of objects above it (transparent everywhere else). Useful for product viewers on a transparent background:
Drei: <ShadowMaterial> on a plane.
Vanilla: new THREE.ShadowMaterial({ opacity: 0.5 }).
const plane = new THREE.Mesh(
new THREE.PlaneGeometry(20, 20),
new THREE.ShadowMaterial({ opacity: 0.4 })
);
plane.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2;
plane.receiveShadow = true;
scene.add(plane);
